## Overview

Driver drowsiness can put the driver and nearby road users at risk. Many demos stop after detecting closed eyes and playing an alert. This project explores a broader research question:

> **When a system detects driver drowsiness, can it notify nearby simulated vehicles and coordinate a response that helps the affected vehicle reach a safe stop?**

The project connects a live webcam, facial-landmark signals, temporal risk assessment, a driver-state machine, application-level vehicle-to-vehicle (V2V) messages, and simulated vehicle responses. It is designed as a modular Master's research prototype: components can be configured, experiments can be repeated, and events can be inspected after each run.

**Current scope:** the system is demonstrated in software and traffic simulators. It does not control a real vehicle, and detection performance has not been validated as a real-world safety measure using a representative labelled driver dataset.

