## Requirements

| Component | Minimum / expected setup | Recommended setup |
|---|---|---|
| **Operating system** | Windows 10/11 is the primary target described by the project. | Windows 10/11 for the documented CARLA demo. |
| **Python** | Python 3.10 for the CARLA 0.9.15 Windows compatibility path. | Python 3.10 in a dedicated virtual environment. |
| **Camera** | Built-in or USB webcam for live monitoring. | Front-facing camera with stable positioning and even lighting. |
| **GPU** | Not required for the core webcam detector or Mock backend. | NVIDIA GPU with 6 GB+ VRAM for a smoother CARLA 3D experience; actual requirements depend on CARLA settings. |
| **Disk space** | Approximately 2 GB for a reduced setup without CARLA, subject to dependencies. | Approximately 20 GB or more for CARLA and assets; verify against the actual map/assets selected. |

Core stack: Python, OpenCV, MediaPipe, NumPy, Streamlit, pandas, pytest, and optional SUMO/TraCI and CARLA components. Install simulator dependencies by following the repository's setup guides; simulator binaries and Python packages may need separate configuration.

On Windows, the audible alert can use the standard-library `winsound` module. On other platforms, sound behaviour depends on the implementation and installed audio support.

## FAQ

<details>
  <summary><strong>Can I use a normal laptop webcam?</strong></summary>
  <p>Yes, for the prototype's live monitoring pipeline. No dedicated GPU is required for the core webcam detection path, although lighting, camera position, and tracking quality can affect results. CARLA's 3D simulation benefits from a capable GPU.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>How accurate is the drowsiness detection?</strong></summary>
  <p>The quantitative table in this README uses synthetic, labelled sessions. It is not a real-world accuracy estimate. Detection performance has not yet been established on a representative, independently labelled driver-video dataset.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>Can this be installed in a real car?</strong></summary>
  <p>No. This is a research and education prototype. It does not control real steering, brakes, throttle, a CAN bus, or automotive hardware, and it is not a certified safety system.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>Do I need CARLA?</strong></summary>
  <p>No. CARLA is used for the visual 3D demonstration. SUMO is used for traffic-flow experiments, and the Mock backend supports lightweight tests and demonstrations without either simulator. Available features depend on the selected backend.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>Can I replace the detector or simulator?</strong></summary>
  <p>The architecture is designed to support replacement through configuration and simulator adapters. The amount of work depends on the interfaces and behaviour provided by the new component.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>Can I use this for my thesis or a research paper?</strong></summary>
  <p>That depends on the license you purchase and your institution's rules. Cite the project when using it in published work, and clearly distinguish simulation results from real-world validation.</p>
</details>

<details>
  <summary><strong>What do I receive after purchase?</strong></summary>
  <p>The deliverables should be listed in the written quote or license agreement. The intended package may include source code, setup instructions, documentation, tests, and experiment materials, depending on the selected tier.</p>
</details>

## Safety and scope

> **Software simulation and research prototype only.** This project does not control a real vehicle, steering, brakes, throttle, a CAN bus, or automotive hardware. Simulated nearby-vehicle responses are research behaviours, not certified automotive safety behaviour. The V2V layer is an application-level simulation and does not currently claim realistic wireless delay, packet loss, or congestion modelling. The detection metrics shown here come from synthetic labelled sessions and have not been validated as real-world driver-drowsiness accuracy. Do not deploy this prototype in a real vehicle or rely on it for safety-critical decisions.

## Roadmap

- Validate and recalibrate thresholds using appropriately collected, real, labelled driver datasets.
- Add configurable V2V network delay, packet-loss, and congestion models.
- Run multi-seed experiments and report variability and confidence intervals.
- Expand ego-vehicle control for curved roads and additional road geometries.
- Explore per-user calibration for eye and mouth thresholds.

